# Design System

The codebase has a single source of truth for visual tokens. Never go around it.

## Tokens

- Never hardcode colors, spacing, radius, typography, or shadows.
- Always import from `@/theme` (`useThemeColors`, `space`, `typography`, `radius`).
- New visual values get added as tokens first, then used.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD
<View style={{ padding: 16, backgroundColor: '#0A0A0A', borderRadius: 8 }} />

// ✅ GOOD
const palette = useThemeColors();
<View style={{ padding: space.gap, backgroundColor: palette.surfaceElevated, borderRadius: radius.sm }} />
```

## Theme awareness

- Support light and dark mode from day one. Every component subscribes to
  `useThemeColors()` so palette swaps live.
- Verify every component state (idle, hover, pressed, disabled, loading, error)
  in both palettes before merging.

## Typography

- Use the scalable typography system (`textStyles`, `typography.fontSize.*`).
- Honor user font scale; never set absolute pixel sizes outside tokens.

## Accessibility (non-optional)

- Every interactive element gets `accessibilityRole`, `accessibilityLabel`,
  and `accessibilityState` where applicable.
- Live status changes announce via `AccessibilityInfo.announceForAccessibility`.
- Respect `AccessibilityInfo.isReduceMotionEnabled()` — skip non-essential motion.
- Verify focus order, contrast (WCAG AA), and screen-reader output per state.

## Motion

- Animations stay subtle, performant, and on-brand (calm, mechanical).
- Use Reanimated worklets; avoid layout-thrashing JS animations.
- Loading states use proper skeleton placeholders, not blocking spinners.

## Offline & feedback

- Handle offline states gracefully (banner, disabled CTAs).
- Always show loading, success, and error states for any async UI.
